 ### Pagination
 
-List endpoints take `page` (default 1) and `per_page` (default 25, maximum 100). Each list returns `count`
+Every list endpoint supports two styles. A request uses one of them.
+
+**Page numbers** take `page` (default 1) and `per_page` (default 25, maximum 100). Each list returns `count`
 (the total across all pages), `page`, `per_page`, `next_page`, and `previous_page`. The last two are `null`
 when there is no such page. A value that is not a positive whole number returns `422 validation_failed`.
+Counting the whole list gets slower as it grows, so for long histories use cursors.
+
+**Cursors** take `page[size]` (default 25, maximum 100). `page[after]=<cursor>` gives the records after a position,
+and `page[before]=<cursor>` gives the records before it. Send neither for the first page:
+
+    GET /api/v1/tickets?page[size]=25&status=open
+    GET /api/v1/tickets?page[size]=25&status=open&page[after]=<meta.after_cursor>
+
+A cursor page returns the records with `meta` and `links`, and no `count`:
+
+    {"tickets": [...],
+     "meta": {"has_more": true, "after_cursor": "...", "before_cursor": "..."},
+     "links": {"next": "https://.../api/v1/tickets?page%5Bafter%5D=...&page%5Bsize%5D=25&status=open", "prev": null}}
+
+- **Cursors for the first and last records.** `meta.after_cursor` is the cursor of the page's last record, and
+  `meta.before_cursor` is the cursor of its first record. A page with no records has `null` for both and `null` links.
+- **Links** lead to the neighbouring pages in either direction. They keep the filters and the page size, and are
+  `null` when there is no such page.
+- **`has_more`** says whether more records lie in the direction being browsed: after the page for `page[after]`
+  and the first page, and before it for `page[before]`.
+- **Cursors are opaque.** Each one is signed and belongs to one business and one endpoint. A cursor from another list,
+  or one that was altered, returns `422 validation_failed` under `page[after]` or `page[before]`. Filters are not part
+  of a cursor, so a client can change them between pages. Cursors stay valid until the server's `SECRET_KEY_BASE`
+  changes. After that, start again from the first page.
+- **Refused combinations** return `422 validation_failed`: `page[...]` with `page` or `per_page`, both `page[after]` and
+  `page[before]`, and an unknown `page[...]` option. A `page[size]` that is not a positive whole number is also refused.
+
+**Stability while others work.** A list keeps its order: tickets and requests by most recent activity, comments oldest
+first, and everything else by ID. A cursor names a position in that order, not a row count, so records added while you
+browse do not shift or repeat the pages you read. Activity moves a ticket to the top of the list. A ticket that changes
+before you reach it can therefore be missed going forward, and can reappear going back, just as it would in the list
+itself. Deleting a record does not break a cursor.
 
